    {'para': '**124. Verbs. Unaccented ⟨ya⟩-class.** Roots of this class form their present-stem by adding ⟨ya⟩ to the root, which bears the accent. Thus from ⟨nah⟩ is made the present-stem ⟨nahya⟩; from ⟨lubh⟩, ⟨lubhya⟩.'},
    {'para': '**125.** The inflection of stems of this class follows the model of ⟨vad⟩.'},
    {'para': '**126.** Certain ⟨ā⟩-roots, because of their peculiar exchanges with ⟨i⟩ and ⟨ī⟩-forms, especially in the formation of the present-stem, are given by the Hindu grammarians as ending in ⟨e⟩ or ⟨ai⟩ or ⟨o⟩ (cf. § 132), and by them assigned to the ⟨bhū⟩, or ⟨a⟩-class. Thus ⟨dhā⟩, ‘suck’ (Hindu ⟨dhe⟩), forms ⟨dhayati⟩; the root ⟨hū⟩ or ⟨hvā⟩ (Hindu ⟨hve⟩) forms ⟨hvayati⟩; ⟨gā⟩ (Hindu ⟨gai⟩) makes ⟨gāyati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**127.** For the root ⟨dṛś⟩, ‘see’, is substituted in the present-system another root ⟨paś⟩, which makes ⟨paśyati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**128. Masculines in ⟨u⟩.** ⟨bhānu⟩, ‘sun’.'},
